# Updating this vendored tree

Do this whenever `venus-protocol` is re-vendored from a newer Mesa/virglrenderer release. Every step
matters — skipping one is how this crate would end up compiling against headers whose generated
switch statement, or whose blob-storage aliasing behaviour, has silently drifted out from under it.

1. **Re-copy the headers.** Replace everything under `vendor/venus-protocol/` with the new release's
   generated `vn_protocol_renderer_*.h` files (plus whatever Vulkan headers they pull in — currently
   `vulkan.h`, `vulkan_core.h`, `vk_platform.h`, `vk_video/*.h`). Do **not** hand-edit anything you
   copy in: this file's own top line records which upstream release the copy came from, and
   `build.rs`'s comment explains why `csrc/` must still resolve `vkr_cs.h` before this directory does.
   Update the version line at the top of this file to the new release.

2. **Regenerate the switch.** Run `python3 tools/gen_switch.py > csrc/decode_switch.inc` from the
   crate root and inspect the diff. A clean re-vendor should mostly add or rename `case` arms for new
   or renamed command types; a case disappearing entirely is worth understanding before committing,
   since it means this crate can no longer size a command it previously could.

3. **Re-run the crate's own tests**, `CARGO_TARGET_DIR=/home/roland/.cache/rayland-c1-target cargo
   test -p rayland-venus-proto` and `cargo test -p rayland-vtest`, and specifically confirm
   `the_size_table_and_the_borrowed_decoder_agree` (in `rayland-vtest`'s `venus_ring::decode` tests)
   still passes — it is the cross-check that the hand-derived fixed-size table and Mesa's own
   generated decoder still agree on the commands both can size. A failure here means either the new
   Mesa release changed a wire encoding this crate hard-codes in `encoded_size`, or this crate's own
   understanding of that encoding was wrong all along; either way, do not silence the test to get a
   green build.

4. **Re-do the write-audit behind the SAFETY comment on `command_len`** (`src/lib.rs`) and the
   matching comment on `vkr_cs_decoder_get_blob_storage` (`csrc/vkr_cs.h`). Both currently assert that
   no generated `_temp` decoder at virglrenderer 1.2.0 ever writes through the pointer
   `vkr_cs_decoder_get_blob_storage` hands back — every call site immediately does
   `if (!p) return; vn_decode_blob_array(dec, p, size)`, and that function's own same-address
   special-case turns the "copy" into a no-op. A newer Mesa release could add a command whose
   generated decoder writes through this pointer for a real reason (not just a same-address copy),
   which would silently mutate the Rust caller's `&[u8]` — undefined behaviour, and on the
   `RAYLAND_RING_DUMP` path, a diagnostic-becomes-corruption bug. Re-grep the new tree for every
   caller of `vkr_cs_decoder_get_blob_storage`/`vkr_cs_encoder_get_blob_storage` and re-confirm the
   same pattern holds before trusting those two comments again.

5. **Re-check the license split**, `vendor/LICENSE.mesa`: confirm the new release's Mesa-authored
   `vn_protocol_renderer_*.h` files are still MIT and the Khronos Vulkan headers are still
   Apache-2.0 (each file's own `SPDX-License-Identifier` line is the source of truth; `LICENSE.mesa`
   is a convenience summary, not a substitute for checking it).
